### Working Group Drafts

#### LISP-DDT: from Experimental to Standards Track
* **Presentation**: Presented by Loránd Jakab ([LISP-DDT: from Experimental to Standards Track](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lisp-lisp-ddt-from-experimental-to-standards-track-01)), discussing `draft-ietf-lisp-8111bis`.
* **Details**:
  * Revision -02 addresses reviews, transitioning LISP-DDT to Standards Track.
  * Removed Database ID and extended the Instance ID field from 24 bits to 32 bits (Extended EID structure now comprises Instance ID, AFI, and EID prefix).
  * Requested IANA to make the Type 6 entry permanent and added DDT action code registries.
  * Added a deployment history section documenting multi-vendor interoperability (OpenLISP, Cisco OS), 7 root node organizations, 9 independent networks, and the 2012 cutover from BGP-based mapping.
* **Discussion**:
  * Marc Portoles Comeras inquired about mapping the 32-bit Instance ID to 24-bit data plane headers. Luigi Iannone clarified keeping 32 bits in DDT control plane is appropriate. Dino Farinacci suggested documenting the control/data plane mapping details in `draft-ietf-lisp-vpn` or `draft-ietf-lisp-rfc8060bis`.

#### LISP YANG Model
* **Presentation**: Presented by Alberto Rodriguez-Natal ([LISP YANG Update -26](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lisp-lisp-yang-update-26-00)), covering `draft-ietf-lisp-yang`.
* **Details**:
  * Revision -26 updates Instance ID length to 32 bits to match `draft-ietf-lisp-rfc8060bis`.
  * Updated Autonomous System number representations (IPv4/IPv6), UDP ports, Explicit Locator Path (ELP), source-destination rules, key-value pairs, and added rejection of empty encapsulation bit sets.
  * Final completion is waiting for `draft-ietf-lisp-rfc8060bis` to incorporate updated geo-coordinates format.

#### LISP Site External Connectivity
* **Presentation**: Presented by Prakash Jain ([LISP Site External Connectivity](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lisp-lisp-site-external-connectivity-00)), covering `draft-ietf-lisp-site-external-connectivity`.
* **Details**:
  * Added an AI fabric infrastructure use case while keeping core protocol flows unchanged.
  * Highlighted Cisco SD-Access deployments leveraging auto-provisioning for external connectivity and fast convergence upon 0.0.0.0/0 default route failure via PubSub.

