### Non-Working Group Items & Use Cases

#### LISP-Based Network for AI Infrastructure
* **Presentation**: Presented by Prakash Jain ([LISP-Based Network for AI Infrastructure](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lisp-lisp-based-network-for-ai-infrastructure-00)).
* **Details**:
  * Outlined network requirements for AI training/inference across scale-up (intra-pod non-IP memory operations, ~2k GPUs), scale-out (inter-pod data center RDMA over IP, ~100k GPUs), and scale-across (inter-data center WAN, up to 500k-1M GPUs).
  * LISP provides identity/locator separation enabling non-IP addressing for scale-up networks, unified multi-domain control plane, Instance ID segmentation, dynamic PubSub convergence, and native multicast for in-network collective operations.
* **Discussion**:
  * Dino Farinacci discussed Mixture of Experts (MoE) multicast mapping and flow-based metrics in mapping systems.
  * In chat, Joel Halpern asked about integration between LISP for AI fabrics and Multi-Resource Control (MRC).
  * Chairs requested continuing technical details on the mailing list.

#### LISP Deployment Experience @ NTS
* **Presentation**: Presented by Evelyn Reha ([LISP Deployment experience @ NTS](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lisp-lisp-deployment-experience-nts-01)).
* **Details**:
  * Overview of ~180 SD-Access fabric sites (~3,000 LISP instances, ~55,000 endpoints). Operational data showed fewer than 10 LISP-related issues out of ~200 support tickets.
  * Transitioning from BGP to PubSub significantly improved stability and default route convergence.
  * Identified operational edge cases: small switches overloaded by ~600 LISP instances and layer 2 VNI misconfigurations causing map cache poisoning during cabling loops. Requested a control-plane mechanism to trigger edge node cache clearing.
* **Discussion**:
  * Marc Portoles Comeras noted that recent updates to extended PubSub help mitigate stale map cache entries.

#### LISP Deployment @ Howdens
* **Presentation**: Presented by David Maroney ([LISP Deployement Howdens](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lisp-lisp-deployement-howdens-00)).
* **Details**:
  * Operational experience deploying LISP across manufacturing plants (100-acre site, 100 XTRs, 300+ APs), greenfield warehouses (30 XTRs, 140 APs), and corporate offices.
  * Benefits include IP mobility, seamless wired/wireless roaming, zero-trust segmentation aligned with IEC 62443 / PURDUE models, reduction of L2 fault domains via routed access, dual-uplink active utilization, and telemetry.
  * Primary challenge: Difficulty recruiting network engineers experienced with LISP.

#### LISP as a Substrate for AI Agent Communication
* **Presentation**: Presented by Wei Wang ([Using LISP as a Network Substrate for AI Agent Communication](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lisp-using-lisp-as-a-network-substrate-for-ai-agent-communication-00)).
* **Details**:
  * Distributed AI agents require location-independent identity, group isolation, and capability-aware discovery (e.g., matching latency constraints and compute capabilities).
  * Proposed a Query Expression LCAF (QE LCAF) to allow Map-Requests to carry service capability requirements and constraints for dynamic resolution in the mapping system.

#### Decoupled LISP and EVPN Interconnection
* **Presentation**: Presented by Balaji Venkatachalapathy ([LISP-EVPN-Interconnect-IETF127](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lisp-lisp-evpn-interconnect-ietf127-00)).
* **Details**:
  * Described decoupled L2 and L3 interconnection architectures between LISP enterprise networks and EVPN data centers running VXLAN without requiring wire protocol modifications.
  * Detailed three models: Decoupled L2 handoff over standard Ethernet; Decoupled L3 VRF handoff via BGP/IGP (widely deployed); and Decoupled L3 same-subnet routing exchanging host routes (/32 or /128) with EVPN proxy ARP.
* **Discussion**:
  * In chat, Reshad Rahman asked if the draft was shared with the BESS WG; Balaji Venkatachalapathy confirmed it will be shared. Marc Portoles Comeras noted that foundational drafts `draft-ietf-lisp-vpn` and `draft-ietf-lisp-eid-mobility` should move to Standards Track.

#### LISP Silent Host Discovery
* **Presentation**: Presented by Lugnathan ([lisp-silent-host-discovery-ietf127](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lisp-lisp-silent-host-discovery-ietf127-00)).
* **Details**:
  * Solves discovery of quiet/low-power endpoints without core network flooding.
  * XTRs advertise discovery capabilities using a new Silent Host Discovery LCAF in Map-Register messages. When an unknown destination is queried, the Map Server forwards targeted Map-Requests to registered capable XTRs to initiate local ARP/ND probing.
  * Includes rate-limiting and throttling guardrails to prevent discovery request storms. Intended status: Experimental.
* **Discussion**:
  * Clarified that Map-Requests are sent via unicast replication to capable XTRs rather than multicast.

